Found 2026-08-28 by the k80 agent while measuring GET /images/{id}/history
against Podman 5.8.4 (API 1.44). Not fixed -- it is a property of the DSL,
not of the prose that ticket was about.

The engine answers "Tags": null for a layer that carries no tag.

  ImageHistoryResponseItem->from_data({ ..., Tags => undef })
    ->tags                     undef      -- accepted cleanly
    ->unknown_fields           {}         -- not filed as unrecognised
    ->rejected_fields          {}         -- not rejected
    ->TO_JSON                  no Tags key at all

So an explicit JSON null and an absent field are indistinguishable after a
round trip. TO_JSON omits an unset attribute by design -- Docker tells an
absent flag from a false one, and that rule is right -- but "the engine said
null" and "the engine said nothing" are two different statements, and the
model currently collapses them.

Nothing is red today: a history item is never sent back to the daemon, and
no fixture covers that endpoint. It matters where a response is inflated and
handed on, or compared, and for anything that ever round-trips a response
into a request.

Worth deciding rather than patching: is a JSON null a value the model should
carry, or is it the daemon's way of saying "absent" and correctly collapsed?
Docker's own swagger marks many fields x-nullable, which suggests it means
something. Check what the engine does with an explicit null on the request
side before choosing -- if the daemon treats null as unset, collapsing is
right and this ticket closes as documentation.

Note the same question already has a home: unknown_fields and rejected_fields
both exist to keep what the model cannot represent, and a third case may not
need a third store.

