MEASURED 2026-08-28 against Podman 5.8.4 (API 1.44), as the maintainer asked.
Request side first, with a non-existent image so the daemon parses the body
and then fails the lookup -- nothing was created.

  POST /containers/create
    {"Image":"does-not-exist-xyz-9999"}                 404 no such image
    {"Image":"...","Labels":null}      map             404 no such image
    {"Image":"...","Env":null}         array           404 no such image
    {"Image":"...","AttachStdin":null} bool            404 no such image
    {"Image":"...","HostConfig":null}  object          404 no such image

  and the decisive triple, byte-identical errors all three:
    {}                    500 parsing reference "": repository name must ...
    {"Image":null}        500 parsing reference "": repository name must ...
    {"Image":""}          500 parsing reference "": repository name must ...

So the daemon CANNOT distinguish absent from null. Go's encoding/json
unmarshals null into the type's zero value, and an absent field leaves the
same zero value behind -- "" for a string, nil for a map or slice, false for
a bool. The same holds outbound: a nil slice marshals to null, which is why
history answers "Tags": null rather than omitting it.

CONCLUSION: collapsing null to absent loses no MEANING, and TO_JSON omitting
an unset attribute is right. This ticket is not a bug in the model.

WHAT IT IS INSTEAD -- the claim is wider than the behaviour:

  from_data({ FutureField => undef })->TO_JSON   {"FutureField":null}   kept
  from_data({ Binds       => undef })->TO_JSON   {}                     dropped

An unknown field round-trips its null; a KNOWN field with a null value loses
its key. That is defensible on the measurement above, but the model's POD
says a field is never dropped, without qualification, and t/type_fixture_
passthrough.t asserts "round-trips every key the daemon sent".

And that test cannot see it: it compares only the TOP-LEVEL keys of each item
(keys %$item). The two nulls the fixtures actually contain are nested --
networks_list[0].IPAM.Options and secrets_list[1].Spec.Labels -- so the case
is untested at depth. Same shape as k90: an assertion narrower than the
sentence above it.

THE WORK, then, is precision rather than repair:
  1. State the rule where the invariant is stated: a known field's null is
     treated as unset, BECAUSE the daemon cannot tell the two apart, with
     this measurement named. An unknown field keeps its null.
  2. Deepen the passthrough test to compare nested keys, with the null rule
     written into it explicitly -- deepening it without that would turn the
     two fixture nulls red, and they are correct behaviour.

Done in 1a8799c. No behaviour changed -- the measurement said collapsing null to absent costs no meaning, so what was narrowed is the promise, in four places: Role::Type's from_data (a new =head3 carrying the engine, the version, the three byte-identical request errors and the Go zero-value reasoning), its unknown_fields and TO_JSON paragraphs, and API::Docker::Type's 'Unknown fields survive' section, plus the type-model skill and its dsl reference. Re-measured here: from_data({Options=>{'com.docker.x'=>undef}, IPAM=>{Options=>undef, FutureNested=>undef}}) gives {IPAM:{FutureNested:null},Options:{'com.docker.x':null}} -- known field's null gone, unknown field's null kept, caller-keyed map's null kept. The deepened test names the two legitimate drops in a list asserted with is_deeply, so a third one is red and one of these two ceasing to drop is red too. The demonstration: a nested-only regression in _encode_value (grep on truthiness instead of defined) fails the deepened test on 3 of 24 and passes the old flat one 21/21, naming a value 0 four levels deep and an empty string through a caller-keyed map.
